 markets price Moore's win probability at 97-98 percent"], "rationale": "Moore combines incumbency, a fifteen-point Democratic state lean, and an overwhelming fundraising advantage, while every recent poll and all seven race ratings point to a wide margin. Cox's campaign lacks the resources to close a gap this size.", "watch": "Only an extraordinary late event, such as a major scandal or a sharply shifted national environment, could narrow the race, and even that is unlikely to make it competitive.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Wes Moore (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Wes Moore, a Democrat, seeks a second term against Republican Dan Cox in a state that leans Democratic by about fifteen points. Every major race rating, from the Cook Political Report to Sabato's Crystal Ball, places the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets put Moore's chances near 97 to 98 percent.\n\nRecent polling reinforces that picture. A September University of Maryland, Baltimore County survey of likely voters showed Moore ahead 51 to 24, and an August poll found a similar 27-point gap. Moore has raised more than $15 million to Cox's roughly $240,000, giving him a dominant position on the airwaves. Debate coverage shows both candidates pressed on housing, energy costs, and redistricting, with some voter unease on those issues, but nothing in the evidence suggests a competitive race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 19.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9967, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9939, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9968, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9958, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9956, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9959, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9942, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9961